                    <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22037288" target="_blank" class="text-sm text-[#2A4A7A] hover:underline font-mono">10.5281/zenodo.22037288</a>
                    <span class="schema-badge">schema.org</span>
                </div>

                <h2 class="title-font text-3xl font-bold text-[#1A2A4A] mb-4">Abstract</h2>

                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4" itemprop="abstract">
                    The discovery of the Obelisk, a novel class of circular RNA molecules found in the human gut microbiome, represents a confluence of molecular biology and quantum-biological theory. This article synthesizes the formal definition of Obelisks—their molecular identity, functional characterization, and proposed quantum-biological role—into a comprehensive narrative. It posits that these entities may function as resonant antennas, mediating coherent gut-brain signaling through biophotonic coupling.
                </p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Drawing on recent advances in quantum biology and the emerging literature on Obelisk genetics, the article explores the engineering profile of Obelisks, their evolutionary context, and the operational criteria for their detection, culminating in a unified representation of their hypothesized function within the broader framework of the Enteric Absolute.
                </p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    This synthesis integrates the original Zheludev et al. (2024) discovery with subsequent bioinformatics analyses and the provocative quantum-biological interpretation proposed in the Formal Definition of Obelisks. It further examines the implications of Obelisk presence for our understanding of life itself, challenging conventional definitions and opening new avenues for microbiome research and biotechnology, particularly in the context of the microbiota–gut–brain axis and mental health.
                </p>

